UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 1: Foods, festivals and traditions', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a festival of lights celebrated by many Hindu, Sikh and Jain families, and Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan for Muslim communities. Use trusted sources or ask families, because traditions differ.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA student says that everyone from one country celebrates in exactly the same way. What should they do to correct this?\n\nFEEDBACK\nUse sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways.\n\nWATCH FOR\nUsing one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nComplete a table of three traditions with name, who celebrates, what people do, the meaning and the source. Write one thing the traditions share with another tradition you know.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a festival of lights celebrated by many Hindu, Sikh and Jain families, and Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan for Muslim communities. Use trusted sources or ask families, because traditions differ.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Complete a table of three traditions with name, who celebrates, what people do, the meaning and the source. Write one thing the traditions share with another tradition you know. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways. Reasoning: I used tradition by attending to festival and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. Correction: Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways. Why: the correction restores accurate use of tradition.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating tradition on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with tradition, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways. This matches the lesson skill for tradition.","options":["Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways."],"question":"A student says that everyone from one country celebrates in exactly the same way. What should they do to correct this?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a…","Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways.","Careful use of festival and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named festival and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the festival and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising tradition. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about tradition.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about festival and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example table: Lunar New Year; celebrated by many East and South-East Asian communities; families share meals and give red envelopes;…","Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom.… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Complete a table of three traditions with name, who celebrates, what people do, the meaning and the source. Write one thing the traditions share…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating tradition on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways. Reasoning: I used tradition by attending to festival and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with tradition, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. Correction: Use sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways. Why: the correction restores accurate use of tradition.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w33-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise tradition.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use tradition with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify festival and explain why it matters.","I can practise food independently on a new prompt.","I can notice meaning needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a festival of lights celebrated by many Hindu, Sikh and Jain families, and Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan for Muslim…","options":["A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a festival of lights celebrated by many Hindu, Sikh and Jain families, and Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan for Muslim communities. Use trusted sources or ask families, because traditions differ.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample table: Lunar New Year; celebrated by many East and South-East Asian communities; families share meals and give red envelopes; meaning: new year and good luck. Each row has name, who celebrates, what people do, meaning and source.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA student says that everyone from one country celebrates in exactly the same way. What should they do to correct this?\n\nFEEDBACK\nUse sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways.","explain":"A tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a festival of lights celebrated by many Hindu, Sikh and Jain families, and Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan for Muslim communities. Use trusted sources or ask families, because traditions differ.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Two traditions and their meanings.","caption":"Use sources or ask families.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Lunar New Year</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">meals, red envelopes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Diwali</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">festival of lights</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example table: Lunar New Year; celebrated by many East and South-East Asian communities; families share meals and give r Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example table: Lunar New Year; celebrated by many East and South-East Asian communities; families share meals and give r Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample table: Lunar New Year; celebrated by many East and South-East Asian communities; families share meals and give red envelopes; meaning: new year and good luck. Each row has name, who celebrates, what people do, meaning and source. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Traditions within cultures.","caption":"Avoid stereotypes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Eid al-Fitr</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">end of Ramadan</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not all same</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">families differ</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. Here is a more productive model: Example table: Lunar New Year; celebrated by many East and South-East Asian communities; families share meals and give red envelopes; meaning: new year and good luck. Each row has name, who celebrates, what people do, meaning and source.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUse sources and ask people. Families within a country can celebrate in different ways.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Using one picture of a food or festival to stand for a whole culture is a stereotype. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tradition?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA tradition is something passed down in a family or community, such as a festival, a meal or a custom. Festivals have meanings, for example Diwali is a festival of lights celebrated by many Hindu, Sikh and Jain families, and Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan for Muslim communities. Use trusted sources or ask families, because traditions differ. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 1: Foods, festivals and traditions","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Cultures and communities","week":33,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THI